<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominican War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dominican War of Independence was the war by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> won its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, which had ruled the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> since 1822. Independence was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on 27 February 1844, and Dominican forces repelled a series of Haitian invasions until 1856. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Juan Pablo Duarte</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-del-rosario-sanchez">Francisco del Rosario Sánchez</a> and Matías Ramón Mella are honoured as the founders of the nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dominican-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Dominican War of Independence" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mariano Torrente · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_las_Carreras_ganada_por_el_general_de_divisi%C3%B3n_Pedro_Santana_el_d%C3%ADa_21_de_abril_de_1849,_contra_los_haitianos_mandados_por_el_general_Soulouque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 9 November 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a>, the former captain general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, overthrew Spanish rule, and on 9 February 1822 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>'s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a> entered the city with the support of frontier leaders. Haitian rule grew unpopular for its heavy taxes and an economic crisis caused by the indemnity Haiti had to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1838 Duarte, Mella and Sánchez founded the secret society <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-trinitaria">La Trinitaria</a>, organised in cells of three. On 27 February 1844 some 100 Dominicans seized the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerta-del-conde">Puerta del Conde</a>, and the Haitian garrison surrendered the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In March 1844 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-riviere-herard">Charles Rivière-Hérard</a> sent three columns, totalling 30,000 men, east. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-santana">Pedro Santana</a> fought them at the Battle of Azua on 19 March, General José María Imbert defeated a column at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 30 March, and on 15 April Dominican schooners sank three Haitian ships off Azua. Hérard was ousted on 3 May, and in July Santana took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, pushing the Trinitarios aside. A second Haitian campaign in 1845 was beaten back at Estrelleta and the Battle of Beler.</p><p>In March 1849 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faustin-soulouque">Faustin Soulouque</a> invaded with 10,000 troops and burned Azua, but Santana routed his army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-carreras">Battle of Las Carreras</a> on 21 and 22 April. Late that year Dominican squadrons bombarded and burned Haitian coastal villages. After a truce mediated in 1851 by the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> broke down, Soulouque, now Emperor Faustin I, invaded in November 1855 with 30,000 men. José María Cabral defeated 12,000 Haitians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-santome">Battle of Santomé</a> on 22 December 1855, and in January 1856 some 8,000 Dominicans defeated 22,000 Haitians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sabana-larga">Battle of Sabana Larga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> kept Lascahobas and Hinche. A further invasion was defeated in 1859, but with the country ruined and fearing new attacks, Santana annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1861. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-restoration-war">Dominican Restoration War</a> that followed cost Spain thousands of dead and wounded and many more to yellow fever, and Spain withdrew in 1865. Haiti recognised Dominican independence in 1867, and the frontier was settled only in 1936.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dominican War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the second major battle of the <b>Dominican War of Independence</b>, outnumbered Dominican troops of the Army of the North under General José María Imbert defeated Haitian troops under General Jean-Louis Pierrot at Santiago de los Caballeros.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
